{#
  The shared skeleton. Both reports open on the same cover, and the cover is
  where the caveats live rather than in a footnote: how old the evidence is,
  how much of it reached a verdict, and what could not be read at all. A reader
  who stops after page one has still been told what the numbers are worth.
#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{ title }} — {{ report.organization.name }}</title>
{# `safe` because the stylesheet is this package's own file. Escaped, its
   quotes become entities a <style> element never decodes, and every quoted
   value -- the font stack, the running footer -- is silently dropped. #}
<style>{{ css | safe }}</style>
</head>
<body class="report-{{ report.kind }}">

<div class="cover">
  <div class="cover-band">
    <table class="cover-top">
      <tr>
        <td class="brand">
          {# The product's shield mark, drawn inline: a report carries no
             image anything has to fetch. #}
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
            <path fill="currentColor" opacity="0.18" d="M12 2 4 5.5v6c0 4.6 3.2 8.9 8 10.5 4.8-1.6 8-5.9 8-10.5v-6L12 2Z" />
            <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 2.9 4.8 6.1v5.4c0 4.2 2.9 8.1 7.2 9.6 4.3-1.5 7.2-5.4 7.2-9.6V6.1L12 2.9Z" />
            <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="m8.8 12.2 2.2 2.2 4.2-4.4" />
          </svg>CloudGuard
        </td>
        <td class="classification"><span>Confidential</span></td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="eyebrow">Cloud security posture assessment</p>
    <div class="accent"></div>
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    <p class="org">{{ report.organization.name }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="cover-body">
    <table class="meta">
      <tr>
        <td>
          <span class="label">Prepared for</span>
          <span class="value">{{ report.organization.name }}</span>
        </td>
        <td>
          <span class="label">Generated</span>
          <span class="value">{{ report.generated_at | datetime }}</span>
        </td>
        <td class="wide">
          <span class="label">Evidence collected</span>
          <span class="value">
          {% if report.evidence.newest_reading_at %}
            {{ report.evidence.oldest_reading_at | datetime }} to
            {{ report.evidence.newest_reading_at | datetime }}
          {% else %}
            —
          {% endif %}
          </span>
        </td>
        <td>
          <span class="label">Activity window</span>
          <span class="value">Last {{ report.window_days }} days</span>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>

{#
  Said before any number, not after them. A posture is a reading of a moment,
  and a report that leads with 84/100 and mentions three-week-old evidence in
  an appendix has already been believed by then.
#}
{% set stale = report.evidence.stale_hours and report.evidence.stale_hours > 48 %}
{% set partial = report.evidence.coverage_ratio is not none and report.evidence.unknown > 0 %}
{% if stale or partial or report.evidence.unclassified_risks > 0
      or report.omitted_sections or report.evidence.collection_errors %}
    <div class="read-first">
      <p class="eyebrow">Read this first</p>
      <h2>What these numbers are worth</h2>

{% if stale %}
      <div class="note note-warn">
        <p class="note-title">This report describes an environment that has not been read recently</p>
        <p class="small">
          The oldest reading behind these numbers is
          {{ report.evidence.stale_hours | round(0) | int }} hours old. Anything changed
          in your cloud since then is not represented here.
        </p>
      </div>
{% endif %}

{% if partial %}
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note-title">
          {{ (report.evidence.coverage_ratio * 100) | round(0) | int }}% of checks reached a verdict
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          {{ report.evidence.unknown }} check{{ '' if report.evidence.unknown == 1 else 's' }}
          could not be evaluated — CloudGuard could not collect what they read.
          An unevaluated check is never counted as a pass, so it is neither a problem
          reported here nor a clean result.
        </p>
      </div>
{% endif %}

{% if report.evidence.unclassified_risks > 0 %}
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note-title">
          {{ report.evidence.unclassified_risks }} of
          {{ report.evidence.unclassified_risks + report.evidence.classified_risks }}
          open risks sit on assets of unknown business context
        </p>
        <p class="small">
          CloudGuard could not establish how critical those assets are or how
          sensitive the data they hold. They are ranked here as though they matter, so
          nothing important is hidden behind a missing label — but the security score
          is charged only for what was established, and it will move once these
          environments are described.
        </p>
      </div>
{% endif %}

{#
  What this document does not contain. A reader handed a report with no
  compliance section should be told it was left out rather than conclude that
  CloudGuard assesses no frameworks — an omission somebody chose looks exactly
  like an absence of evidence once the PDF is forwarded.
#}
{% if report.omitted_sections %}
      <div class="note">
        <p class="note-title">Sections excluded from this report</p>
        <p class="small">
          {{ report.omitted_sections | join(', ') | capitalize }}.
          They were not included when this document was generated; nothing here says
          anything about them either way.
        </p>
      </div>
{% endif %}

{% if report.evidence.collection_errors %}
      <div class="note note-warn">
        <p class="note-title">Part of the environment could not be read</p>
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for scope, reason in report.evidence.collection_errors.items() %}
            <tr><td>{{ scope }}</td><td class="small">{{ reason }}</td></tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
{% endif %}
    </div>
{% endif %}

{#
  What is in this document, with the page each part starts on. Listed from the
  same conditions the body branches on, so the contents can never promise a
  section the reader then cannot find.
#}
    <div class="contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">Contents</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#posture">Security posture</a></li>
        {% if 'top_risks' in report.sections and (report.kind == 'executive' or report.top_risks) %}
        <li><a href="#top-risks">What is worst right now</a></li>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'attack_paths' in report.sections %}
        <li><a href="#attack-paths">Attack paths</a></li>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'remediation' in report.sections %}
        <li><a href="#remediation">Remediation progress</a></li>
        {% endif %}
        {% if 'compliance' in report.sections %}
        <li><a href="#compliance">Compliance coverage</a></li>
        {% endif %}
        {% if report.kind == 'technical' and 'findings' in report.sections %}
        <li><a href="#findings">Open findings</a></li>
        {% endif %}
        <li><a href="#about">How to read this report</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="body-start">
{% block body %}{% endblock %}
</div>

{#
  How to read the document, printed in it. A PDF is read by people who have
  never opened CloudGuard, and the distinctions this product insists on --
  finding against risk, claimed against verified, no verdict against a pass --
  are exactly the ones a reader would otherwise guess at. Nothing here is a
  number: every figure is in the sections above, measured.
#}
<div class="about">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="about">How to read this report</h2>

  <dl class="terms">
    <dt>Security score</dt>
    <dd>
      A reading of the environment at its last scan, from 0 to 100, higher being
      better. It starts at 100 and every open risk takes something off, the worst
      the most: two open critical risks alone leave 60. It is never flat, so every
      verified fix moves it.
    </dd>
    <dt>Finding and risk</dt>
    <dd>
      A finding is what CloudGuard observed. A risk is what that observation
      means for this asset, with this data, at this level of exposure — which is
      why risks are ranked by score rather than by severity alone.
    </dd>
    <dt>Verified fixed</dt>
    <dd>
      A later scan observed the check passing. CloudGuard never closes a finding
      because somebody said it was fixed.
    </dd>
    <dt>Accepted risk</dt>
    <dd>
      A decision to live with a finding. It is still present in the environment,
      and is counted neither as open nor as fixed.
    </dd>
    <dt>No verdict</dt>
    <dd>
      <span class="sev sev-UNKNOWN">Unknown</span> — the check could not read what
      it needed. It is never a pass, and never counted as one.
    </dd>
    <dt>Attack path</dt>
    <dd>
      A route from something reachable from the internet to something sensitive,
      built from what CloudGuard observed. Fewer hops is worse.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</div>

</body>
</html>
